A||||8||Homicidal||Is this the correct sub Footwell install?||||5||
Z||000000||Homicidal||08-07-2000||03:17 AM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/MotorCity/Track/1335/audiopic1.jpg"> <P>If so, I might think about it!  What holds the front panel to the sides?  Is that enough air for the sub?<P>How much approx.  for the install if I do the handywork?||||reg||5
Z||000001||stimpy||08-07-2000||07:45 AM||||I'm sure that is a correct installation. Mine looks different. I handed off the pic to an inlaw to scan yesterday and thought I'd have it this am, hopfully when I get in this afernoon.  That looks a lot like I imagine philphine's set up looks like.That looks like an 8". Mine is similar, except uses the Polk 10. (And yes, the enclosure has <B>plenty</B> of room for the Polk). That enclosue looks like t goes on a diagonal. Mine goes straight from where the console area flares in to the dead pedal on the right side of the footwell. (It's true, a picture is worth 1000 words!)<P>Putting it together is easy, although a little messy. the contour of the footwell is all fiberglass. I started out by making a form with posterboard and duct tape. I filled that with sprayfoam to make a rigid plug. (ou side of the car, of course. Don't let that stuff get on your car anywhere, it's nasty!) <BR>Next, get a Bondo brand fiberglass repair kit at your local Walmart and got to town on the backside of your plug. you may want to cover the plug with hefty sack so the glass can come off easily, I applied it directly to the posterboard and chipped out all the foam later. It was messy, but it did the job.<BR>When laying the glass, use small strips of the cloth and lots of resin. Only mix up enough resin to do the part that you are laying, but you'll be suurprised how thirsty that stuff is. If it starts pulling up, it's too dry. Do not try to flatten it out, as you'll pull the hell out of it. Let it dry.<BR>It will take a few days to lay all the glass and let it cure right. After all is done on the footwell form, make the face out of mdf (I used 3/8" plywood and it works fine).<BR>lay the back on the face and draw a line around it that wll be parrallel to the face,and grind down to that line. Then glass the front to the back. It is not necessary to lay glass all over the face, just wrap around the front. <BR>As far as staying anchored in place, friction does the trick. The enclosure should fit tight like a cork in a bottle. If you want, use some Velcro hook fasteners to get it to stick to your carpet<P>I swear I have pics coming!!!! <BR><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://gifs123.tripod.com/page15/renstimpy2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||1FST2M6||08-07-2000||08:19 AM||||yeah that looks right to me.. I've been diggin through a bunch of crap toget the sizes i used. what is the volume of that box? if the volume of the box is what the manufacturer of the sub suggest then yeah its fine.. if its to shallow what pack it with polyfil tht will trick the speaker into thinking its in a larger box by cushioning the cone.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Philphine||08-07-2000||09:38 AM||||yep, mine looks about like that. maybe smaller (probaly too small but i guesstimated). slight difference in method though. i pretty much taped up the area with duct tape, sprayed the area with spray lubricant (like wd40) and laid fiber glass on the taped up area. stimpy's right about the glass. try to cover every thing in the area that shouldn't get glassed 'cause it's hard or impossible to get off. the front and top are 3/4 mdf. since i molded it right to the footwell it sits really snug. three self taping screws keep it in place. i cut the carpet where the box is and used a matching piece of carpet to cover the front. got a grill from radio shack and painted it gray.<P>WARNING: if you feel around the top of where the center console meets the front footboard the center console has two layers of metal. at the bottom there is just one so if you drill a hole there and go through one layer STOP. the next layer you feel is the gas tank. how do i know? never you mind, just watch out.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Homicidal||08-07-2000||01:02 PM||||Okay, so you build an enclosure to FIT the footwell!?<P>I thought you free aired the sub and had just a face on it!<P>Okay, so I make a enclosure out of fiberglass to fit the footwell?  Anybody got any dimensions I can use?  And pics!  Oh god I NEED PICS!||||reg||1
Z||000005||stimpy||08-07-2000||07:36 PM||||Patience grasshopper, soon images will come...<BR>As far as dimensions go, you really want to just make a mold of the footwell and go from there. But if I remember right, 11"h x18"w x 8"d at it's deepest. This will definately be big enough for the Polk. I would not recommend using a lesser speaker as it would likely self destruct. I went the polyfill route and my box is a bit on the boomy side if I give too much gain. I can only imagine what FST2M6 sounded lie w/ the solabaric and the xtant. Whoo-wee!<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://gifs123.tripod.com/page15/renstimpy2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Black88GT||08-07-2000||10:50 PM||||There really isn't a right way or a wrong way to install the footwell sub.  There IS a bad way and a good way.  Judging from that, it looks like the cheap way.  That sub looks 12" to me, and the box looks way to small for a 12", even a 10".  There is no way it has enough room to breathe, unless he somehow ported it.  The best way is to build a fiberglass enclosure.  I had my friend build me one, when you install it and use stock carpeting to cover it, you can't even see it.  I will post pictures after I get it installed.<P><BR>jeff||||reg||1
Z||000007||86SE||08-07-2000||11:20 PM||||Wait a minute............it is a fiberglass enclosure, it is a 10", and it is big enough, plus a sub does not necessicarily NEED to breathe.  Thats the purpose of a SEALED enclosure, to be sealed.  Just fill the small box with a few pounds of polyfill and there you go, it will sound like its in a bigger box.<P>Oh yeah, thank you very much stimpy!!  Now im going to make a 'real' footwell sub box, just dident know how you did it........sounds fairly easy..   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>High-Output V-6 Yellow Fiero||||reg||3
Z||000008||stimpy||08-08-2000||07:53 AM||||No problem, I am here merely to serve. If you'd like a journal of my project, check out this thread from when I was building it... <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/002401.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/002401.html</A> <BR><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://gifs123.tripod.com/page15/renstimpy2.gif"> ||||reg||1
